  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Switchboard",
    "overview": "The terminal is a line and every Claude account is a jack on a walnut board. Exactly one jack is patched in, and a red cord runs from the terminal line's brass plug down into that jack's socket. The whole system exists to make that one fact, which account Claude Code is using right now, readable at a glance and changeable in one click.\n\nThe material is an old operator's console: a walnut-brown shell, cream label cards that read like typed index tags, brass for rims, rules and the controls you press, and a near-black plate for the terminal line. Names, emails, times and paths are typed in Courier Prime as if on a label maker; everything the interface itself says (buttons, hints, states) is in Archivo. Density is calm: a handful of cards in an auto-filling grid, one sticky footer, no sidebars. Controls are standard native widgets dressed in the board's materials, not reinvented.\n\nThe system refuses the grey settings list with avatar circles. Accounts are physical jacks with sockets and number keys, not rows.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "One cord red, reserved for the live connection.",
      "Cream label cards on a walnut board; a dark plate for the terminal line in both color schemes.",
      "Courier Prime for data, Archivo for interface text.",
      "Brass for every rim, rule and pressable surface on the board.",
      "The patch cord is the only signature motion."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Cord Rule",
        "body": "Cord red appears only where the live connection is: the cord, the live socket, the live ring, the \"Connected\" label. Errors and destructive actions use Alert Salmon, never cord red. If a screen has two red things that are not the same connection, one of them is wrong.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Brass Is Metal Rule",
        "body": "Brass marks things you could touch on a real board: rims, plugs, rules, buttons. It is not a text color for prose on the board.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Typed Label Rule",
        "body": "If the user named it, owns it, or a machine reports it (account, email, plan, time, path, key number), it is set in Courier Prime. If the interface is speaking, it is Archivo.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Flat Empty Jack Rule",
        "body": "The empty jack has no shadow and no fill: a dashed outline on the bare board. Only accounts that exist are raised.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do keep cord red for the live connection only (cord, live socket rim, live ring, \"Connected\").",
      "Do set account names, emails, plans, times, paths and key numbers in Courier Prime; set interface text in Archivo.",
      "Do keep the terminal line and menu on the dark plate in both color schemes.",
      "Do give every jack a socket, a number key cap, and its state as text plus a dot, not color alone.",
      "Do use stroke SVG icons at 18px, 1.75 stroke, from the inline sprite.",
      "Do switch off all animation and transition under `prefers-reduced-motion`."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't render accounts as a grey settings list with avatar circles.",
      "Don't use cord red for errors, warnings or destructive actions; use Alert Salmon.",
      "Don't raise the empty jack; it stays a flat dashed outline.",
      "Don't add a second signature motion; the cord redraw is the only one.",
      "Don't use Anthropic's logo or brand marks anywhere in the interface."
    ]
  }
}
